## Proof

We determine the spectrum $\sigma(B)$ of the Volterra-type integration operator
$$Bx(t)=\int_{-1}^{t}x(\tau)\,d\tau,\qquad B:C_0[-1,1]\to C_0[-1,1],$$
where $C_0[-1,1]=\{x\in C[-1,1]:x(-1)=0\}$ with the sup norm $\|x\|_\infty$.

### Step 1. $B$ is well-defined and bounded

For $x\in C_0[-1,1]$, the map $t\mapsto\int_{-1}^{t}x(\tau)\,d\tau$ is continuous, and at $t=-1$ it equals $0$, so $Bx\in C_0[-1,1]$. Moreover
$$\|Bx\|_\infty=\sup_{t\in[-1,1]}\left|\int_{-1}^{t}x(\tau)\,d\tau\right|\le \int_{-1}^{1}|x(\tau)|\,d\tau\le 2\,\|x\|_\infty,$$
so $B$ is bounded with $\|B\|\le 2$.

### Step 2. $B$ is compact

For $\|x\|_\infty\le 1$ we have $|Bx(t)|\le 2$ (uniform boundedness) and, for $s,t\in[-1,1]$,
$$|Bx(t)-Bx(s)|=\left|\int_{s}^{t}x(\tau)\,d\tau\right|\le |t-s|,$$
so the image of the unit ball is equicontinuous. By the Arzelà–Ascoli theorem, $B$ is compact.

### Step 3. $B$ has no nonzero eigenvalues

Suppose $Bx=\lambda x$ with $\lambda\ne 0$ and $x\in C_0[-1,1]$. Then
$$\int_{-1}^{t}x(\tau)\,d\tau=\lambda\,x(t).$$
The left side is $C^1$, so $x$ is $C^1$. Differentiating gives $x(t)=\lambda\,x'(t)$, i.e. $x'(t)=\frac{1}{\lambda}x(t)$. The general solution is $x(t)=Ce^{t/\lambda}$. Evaluating the original equation at $t=-1$:
$$0=\int_{-1}^{-1}x(\tau)\,d\tau=\lambda\,x(-1)\implies x(-1)=0.$$
Since $x(-1)=Ce^{-1/\lambda}=0$, we get $C=0$, hence $x\equiv 0$. Thus no $\lambda\ne 0$ is an eigenvalue.

### Step 4. The spectrum is $\{0\}$

For a compact operator on a Banach space, every nonzero spectral value is an eigenvalue. Since $B$ has no nonzero eigenvalues (Step 3), the only possible spectral values are $0$ and eigenvalues; hence $\sigma(B)\subseteq\{0\}$.

It remains to confirm $0\in\sigma(B)$. A compact operator on an infinite-dimensional Banach space is never invertible (its image of the unit ball is relatively compact, hence not the whole unit ball), so $B$ is not bijective; in particular $0\in\sigma(B)$.

(Equivalently, the spectral radius is $0$: iterating,
$$B^{n}x(t)=\int_{-1}^{t}\frac{(t-\tau)^{n-1}}{(n-1)!}\,x(\tau)\,d\tau,\qquad \|B^{n}\|\le \frac{2^{n}}{n!}\xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{}0,$$
so $r(B)=\lim_{n\to\infty}\|B^{n}\|^{1/n}=0$, which forces $\sigma(B)=\{0\}$.)

### Conclusion

$$\boxed{\sigma(B)=\{0\}.}$$

